1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:02,710
SpaceX has already caught the booster.

2
00:00:02,750 --> 00:00:05,990
Now Elon Musk says Flight 15 will
try to catch the spacecraft too—

3
00:00:06,030 --> 00:00:08,550
after it comes back from orbit.

4
00:00:08,590 --> 00:00:10,470
He announced it on October 20:

5
00:00:10,510 --> 00:00:12,920
both stages, one mission.

6
00:00:13,006 --> 00:00:14,870
Super Heavy is the booster underneath.

7
00:00:14,910 --> 00:00:17,137
Starship is the spacecraft on top.

8
00:00:17,177 --> 00:00:19,670
They launch together,
then return separately.

9
00:00:19,710 --> 00:00:21,990
The hard part is that second catch.

10
00:00:22,030 --> 00:00:25,320
Miss badly, and the rocket
could take the catcher with it.

11
00:00:25,870 --> 00:00:27,740
Look closely at that first catch.

12
00:00:27,950 --> 00:00:30,720
The booster is still firing
its engines on the way in.

13
00:00:31,010 --> 00:00:34,490
Those engines remove almost all of its
downward speed before the arms take

14
00:00:34,530 --> 00:00:35,430
the load.

15
00:00:35,510 --> 00:00:38,580
The arms support dedicated features
near the top of the vehicle.

16
00:00:38,790 --> 00:00:41,440
They aren't crushing a steel
fuel tank until it sticks.

17
00:00:41,870 --> 00:00:44,560
Think of lowering a heavy
object onto two shelves.

18
00:00:44,790 --> 00:00:46,940
Line up the supports, arrive gently,

19
00:00:47,130 --> 00:00:48,100
transfer the weight.

20
00:00:48,450 --> 00:00:52,000
Sounds reasonable, except
the object is a rocket,

21
00:00:52,040 --> 00:00:55,470
the shelves move, and the crane
is the rocket's own engines.

22
00:00:55,860 --> 00:00:58,210
Being between the arms is only the start.

23
00:00:58,300 --> 00:01:00,350
Its motion has to be under control.

24
00:01:01,000 --> 00:01:03,530
The ship adds a completely
different problem:

25
00:01:03,820 --> 00:01:04,830
orbital speed.

26
00:01:05,180 --> 00:01:07,690
Coming home means getting
rid of that energy.

27
00:01:08,040 --> 00:01:11,260
The atmosphere does much of the braking
while the gas around the vehicle

28
00:01:11,300 --> 00:01:13,010
becomes intensely hot.

29
00:01:13,320 --> 00:01:15,910
That's what the black-tiled
side is protecting it from.

30
00:01:16,510 --> 00:01:18,700
The ship has to hold
the right orientation,

31
00:01:18,830 --> 00:01:20,680
keep its heat shield facing the flow,

32
00:01:20,810 --> 00:01:23,540
and survive with its control
systems still working.

33
00:01:24,190 --> 00:01:27,160
Lower down, Starship descends broadside,

34
00:01:27,230 --> 00:01:30,780
using its body against the air while
the flaps help control its attitude.

35
00:01:31,010 --> 00:01:34,160
Then the engines relight and
swing the vehicle toward upright.

36
00:01:34,430 --> 00:01:38,240
SpaceX has already demonstrated this
flip and landing burn over the ocean.

37
00:01:38,890 --> 00:01:39,880
Watch the change.

38
00:01:41,230 --> 00:01:43,110
Falling sideways becomes a powered,

39
00:01:43,150 --> 00:01:44,660
nearly vertical descent.

40
00:01:44,930 --> 00:01:47,460
The planned tower catch
adds a physical target.

41
00:01:47,650 --> 00:01:49,230
The ship needs the right position,

42
00:01:49,270 --> 00:01:52,620
orientation, and motion when its
support features reach the arms.

43
00:01:53,270 --> 00:01:56,180
Those receiving surfaces
belong to a moving machine.

44
00:01:56,410 --> 00:01:59,840
The arms swing, and their carriage
travels up and down the tower.

45
00:02:00,210 --> 00:02:02,100
Look at the deep trusses underneath.

46
00:02:02,700 --> 00:02:05,850
The load has to pass through
those members into the carriage,

47
00:02:05,920 --> 00:02:07,970
down the tower, and into the ground.

48
00:02:08,570 --> 00:02:10,640
The newer tower uses shorter arms,

49
00:02:10,710 --> 00:02:14,920
and the newer booster has three larger
grid fins with revised catch features.

50
00:02:15,230 --> 00:02:19,730
For a moment, flying machinery and ground
machinery have to become one supported

51
00:02:19,770 --> 00:02:20,670
structure.

52
00:02:21,070 --> 00:02:23,200
Now change one thing at a time.

53
00:02:23,510 --> 00:02:27,340
Add sideways drift, and a support
feature can miss its receiving surface.

54
00:02:28,390 --> 00:02:31,660
Add tilt, and one side can
meet the arms before the other,

55
00:02:31,810 --> 00:02:33,400
changing how the load is shared.

56
00:02:34,450 --> 00:02:36,030
Keep too much downward speed,

57
00:02:36,070 --> 00:02:38,420
and a gentle handoff becomes an impact.

58
00:02:39,470 --> 00:02:42,080
And because the receiving machine
is part of the launch site,

59
00:02:42,250 --> 00:02:45,470
a serious failure could damage the
equipment needed for the next flight

60
00:02:45,510 --> 00:02:46,410
too.

61
00:02:46,670 --> 00:02:50,240
That makes deciding when to attempt
the catch part of the engineering.

62
00:02:50,510 --> 00:02:52,690
A vehicle problem, an engine problem,

63
00:02:52,730 --> 00:02:56,500
or a problem on the ground can remove
the conditions for a safe approach.

64
00:02:56,870 --> 00:03:00,370
Flight 6 already showed that tower
health checks could call off a booster

65
00:03:00,410 --> 00:03:01,190
catch.

66
00:03:01,230 --> 00:03:04,800
Alternatives depend on the mission
plan and when the problem is detected.

67
00:03:04,930 --> 00:03:08,300
They aren't a guaranteed escape
once the rocket is beside the arms.

68
00:03:08,950 --> 00:03:12,630
Moving recovery machinery onto the
ground keeps some of that machinery off

69
00:03:12,670 --> 00:03:13,740
the flying vehicle.

70
00:03:14,050 --> 00:03:18,290
Recovering both stages also creates the
possibility of using the same expensive

71
00:03:18,330 --> 00:03:19,280
hardware again.

72
00:03:19,880 --> 00:03:22,930
That's the prize behind this
ridiculous-looking maneuver.

73
00:03:23,300 --> 00:03:26,610
But bringing a spacecraft home
is the beginning of another job:

74
00:03:26,860 --> 00:03:28,090
checking the heat shield,

75
00:03:28,140 --> 00:03:31,370
engines, and structure, then
proving it can fly again.

76
00:03:31,620 --> 00:03:34,150
Catch it, inspect it, fly it again,

77
00:03:34,260 --> 00:03:35,160
repeat.

78
00:03:35,550 --> 00:03:39,370
The goal is a system that keeps
working rather than a spectacular trick

79
00:03:39,410 --> 00:03:40,460
performed once.

80
00:03:41,610 --> 00:03:43,800
So watch the final seconds differently.

81
00:03:44,400 --> 00:03:46,090
The heat shield has done its job.

82
00:03:46,590 --> 00:03:48,380
The flaps have controlled the descent.

83
00:03:48,980 --> 00:03:51,210
The engines have turned
and slowed the ship.

84
00:03:51,810 --> 00:03:54,560
Now the supports meet, and
the tower takes the load.

85
00:03:56,510 --> 00:03:59,900
That's the sequence SpaceX wants
to complete with both stages.

86
00:04:00,470 --> 00:04:02,420
The first catch brought the booster home.

87
00:04:02,970 --> 00:04:05,210
That's the Flight 15 challenge:

88
00:04:05,250 --> 00:04:06,850
bring both stages home.

89
00:04:06,890 --> 00:04:08,980
Flying them again is the payoff.
